  "articleBody": "Seeing gaps in your molding joints is a pretty common frustration, and it usually boils down to a few key culprits. Often, it's due to poor miter angles or cuts that just aren't precise enough, leading to seams that don't quite line up perfectly. Another big factor is relying too heavily on caulk to hide those inaccurate cuts instead of correcting the fit. Caulk is great for minor imperfections, but it's not a substitute for a tight, well-fitted joint. Sometimes, it's also about the wood itself reacting to changes in temperature and humidity, causing slight expansion or contraction that can open up small gaps over time. In older homes, walls and ceilings aren't always perfectly square, which can make getting tight, seamless molding joints even more challenging. Without careful measurement and adjustment, those imperfections become obvious. Addressing these issues often requires more than just filling a gap; it involves understanding the specific challenges of your home and making precise adjustments. For decorative, stain-grade, or multi-piece molding, especially where the finish quality needs to be top-notch, you really want someone who has experience. If you're noticing these gaps, and you want a professional, polished look, it might be time to bring in an expert. HH Remodeling LLC has the know-how to get those moldings looking seamless and beautiful.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}